<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">At the River (Charles Ives)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">O, hur saligt att få vandra</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1876</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Swedish hymn to the same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Jag har aldrig vart på snusen</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Swedish drinking song to the same melody, partly a parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Welchen Jubel, welche Freude (Ernst Gebhardt)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">German Christmas carol to the same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tabakoya no Musume</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1937</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Japanese enka song to the same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Old American Songs (Aaron Copland)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1952</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">At the River (Anton Plate)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2003</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Symphony No. 9 (David Maslanka)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in a wind band symphony</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mists: Charles Ives for Jazz Orchestra</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2014</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Ives's arrangement arranged for jazz orchestra</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">At the River (Groove Armada)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">trombone part derived from the hymn</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Revelation 22:1–2</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the river of the water of life, its central image</span></li></ul></section>
